---
// Page shell for /personal-finance/budget: intro + the BudgetLab island
// (50/30/20 framework).
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import BudgetLab from "../../components/BudgetLab.tsx";
import "../../styles/simtool.css";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The 50/30/20 Budget"
  description="A simple budgeting framework: aim for 50% of take-home pay on needs, 30% on wants, and 20% to saving and debt. Enter your numbers and see how your split compares — and how much is left to invest."
>
  <section class="container wl-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Personal Finance · Interactive Tool</p>
    <h1>The 50/30/20 budget</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      A budget isn't about restriction — it's about knowing where your money goes so
      you can point some of it at your future. The <strong>50/30/20</strong> rule is
      the simplest starting point: roughly half your take-home pay on needs, a third
      on wants, and a fifth to saving and debt. That last slice is what eventually
      becomes invested. See how your split compares.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-host">
    <BudgetLab client:load />
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-explainer">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What goes where</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Needs (≈50%).</strong> The things you genuinely can't skip: housing,
          utilities, groceries, insurance, basic transportation, and the
          <em>minimum</em> payments on any debt. If needs eat far more than half,
          housing and transportation are almost always the reason — and the biggest
          levers.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Wants (≈30%).</strong> Everything that makes life nicer but you
          could live without: dining out, subscriptions, travel, hobbies, upgrades.
          This is the flexible slice you trim first when you need to free up money.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Save &amp; invest (≈20%).</strong> Building your emergency fund,
          extra debt payoff beyond the minimums, and investing for the future. This
          is the whole point — the slice that connects a budget to a portfolio.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The percentages are a guideline, not a law. High cost-of-living areas push
        needs above 50%; aggressive savers push the last slice well past 20%. What
        matters is that the split is <em>deliberate</em>, and that the save-and-invest
        slice is automatic — money you never see is money you don't spend.
        Educational only, not advice.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container pf-related">
    <h2 class="pf-related-heading">Keep exploring</h2>
    <ul class="grid grid--2 pf-related-grid">
      <li>
        <a href="/personal-finance/debt-payoff" class="card">
          <h3>Put the 20% to work on debt</h3>
          <p>If you're carrying high-interest debt, clearing it is the first place your savings slice should go.</p>
        </a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/tools/compound-growth" class="card">
          <h3>What that slice grows into</h3>
          <p>Feed your monthly savings into the Growth tool and watch decades of compounding do the work.</p>
        </a>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .wl-intro { padding-block: var(--space-lg) var(--space-md); max-width: var(--measure-wide); }
  .lede { font-size: var(--step-1); color: var(--color-text-soft); line-height: 1.5; }
  .wl-explainer { margin-top: var(--space-lg); }
  .pf-related { margin-top: var(--space-xl); }
  .pf-related-heading { font-size: var(--step-1); padding-bottom: var(--space-xs); border-bottom: var(--border); margin-bottom: var(--space-md); }
  .pf-related-grid { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; }
  .pf-related-grid a.card { height: 100%; text-decoration: none; color: inherit; display: block; }
  .pf-related-grid h3 { margin: 0 0 0.3rem; font-size: var(--step-1); }
  .pf-related-grid p { margin: 0; color: var(--color-text-soft); font-size: var(--step--1); }
</style>

<style is:global>
  .ss-headline { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.15rem; padding: var(--space-sm) var(--space-md); border-radius: var(--radius); background: var(--color-accent-soft); border: 1px solid var(--color-accent); }
  .ss-headline-label { font-family: var(--font-sans); font-size: var(--step--1); color: var(--color-text-soft); }
  .ss-headline-value { font-family: var(--font-sans); font-weight: 700; font-size: var(--step-3); line-height: 1.1; color: var(--color-accent); font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  .ss-headline-sub { font-family: var(--font-sans); font-size: var(--step--1); color: var(--color-text-soft); }

  .bl-baraboveL { font-family: var(--font-sans); font-size: var(--step--1); font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-text-soft); margin: var(--space-sm) 0 0.3rem; }
  .bl-bar { display: flex; width: 100%; height: 2.4rem; border-radius: var(--radius); overflow: hidden; border: var(--border); background: var(--color-surface-alt); }
  .bl-bar > div { height: 100%; transition: width var(--transition); }
  .bl-legend { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--space-sm); align-items: center; list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: var(--space-sm) 0 0; font-family: var(--font-sans); font-size: var(--step--1); color: var(--color-text-soft); }
  .bl-legend li { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.4rem; }
  .bl-legend .sw { width: 0.85rem; height: 0.85rem; border-radius: 3px; display: inline-block; }

  .bl-table { display: grid; gap: 0.3rem; margin-bottom: var(--space-md); }
  .bl-trow { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr auto auto auto; gap: 0.8rem; align-items: baseline; padding: 0.3rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border); font-family: var(--font-sans); font-size: var(--step--1); }
  .bl-thead { color: var(--color-muted); font-weight: 700; }
  .bl-num { font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; text-align: right; min-width: 4.5rem; }
  .bl-muted { color: var(--color-muted); }
</style>
